                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; p_1^* \Psi
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{q_1}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{p_1^* \psi}
                  \\\
                  \\Psi&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; Y_1 \times_{X_2} Y_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp;\searrow^f &amp;&amp; {}^{p_1}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{p_2}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; Y_1 &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; Y_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; &amp;&amp; \searrow^u &amp;&amp; {}^v\swarrow &amp;&amp;
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; X_2 &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the above definitions, the push-pull operation
            %math#mathml_113c989f2e9b2a0f6487505e924b173522c83f79_49.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v^* u_*
            is encoded in the pullback property of the total outer rectangle. On the other hand, the pull-push operation
            %math#mathml_113c989f2e9b2a0f6487505e924b173522c83f79_50.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                    %mo *
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {p_2}_* p_1^*
            is determined by the pullback property of the upper square. By the above fact both properties are equivalent. This means that indeed
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_113c989f2e9b2a0f6487505e924b173522c83f79_51.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                    %mo *
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v^* u_* \simeq {p_2}_* p_1^*
          %p
            and hence that the pull-push operations defined by over-categories are compatible with composition of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
          %p
            Finally, there is a simple observation on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} over categories
          %p for
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_113c989f2e9b2a0f6487505e924b173522c83f79_52.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
